                <pb facs="0170" n="58" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg58"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6876: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cunningham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-18T16:00:00+08:00">February 18, 1935—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 19—10:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>73. Major General Kenji Doihara, well-known Japanese militarist arrived from
                    Japan on the 16th. He called yesterday upon Mayor Wu Te-chen and General Huang
                    Fu, chairman of the political affairs readjustment committee of Peiping. He is
                    quoted in the press as emphasizing that all anti-Japanese activities in China
                    must be discontinued before a Sino-Japanese agreement can be reached. Repeated
                    to Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
